1; the rationale's defense (the agreement\n117\tis produced by the epic itself) is reasonable but thin.\n118\t\n119\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (1/2)\n120\tRule-mandated ACs are present where applicable: E002 has a rollback/compatibility AC\n121\t(TP consumers not broken without a deprecation-policy-compliant path), E005 has\n122\tfeature-flag-default and gate-removal ACs, and E006 has the technical-review AC. No\n123\tepic body references sibling epics by draft ID (upstream work is referenced by name,\n124\te.g., \"the support-posture work\"). No externally-controlled soft ACs: E003's\n125\t\"reviewed and accepted by the owning product and support organizations\" is a review of\n126\tthe epic's own deliverable through internal organizations, not an availability\n127\tprecondition granted by outside parties. One slightly vague AC: E004's \"validation\n128\tpass covering the guardrails capabilities the strategy commits to at GA\" — the\n129\tstrategy enumerates no specific guardrails capabilities, so the validation scope is\n130\tcircular as written; it should name the capability list or a concrete validation\n131\tsurface (the epic's own scope section already names one: functional coverage, failure\n132\tbehavior, configuration paths).\n133\t\n134\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n135\tAll HLRs, all three affected components, and the strategy's open question are carried\n136\tforward (the open question becomes E001's deliverable and E002's gate; scattered TP\n137\tfeedback becomes E001's consolidation scope). Out-of-scope statements are respected —\n138\tno new Llama Stack features beyond GA-bar work appear in any epic. Missing strategy\n139\tsections (teams, dependencies, NFRs) are explicitly flagged in Health Warnings with\n140\tthe `.context`-derived team assignments noted for verification. E006's content outline\n141\taccounts for every upstream epic's deliverables (stability guarantees, support\n142\tposture, guardrails integration, Dashboard enablement, TP-c