{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9689f62615090c46","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n10\t\n11\t## Epic List\n12\t\n13\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n14\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n15\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment for Llama Stack | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and GA hardening | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as a supported Llama Stack integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift and TP migration notes | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | Documentation | P0 |\n21\t\n22\t## Dependency DAG\n23\t\n24\t```mermaid\n25\tgraph TD\n26\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 GA-readiness assessment] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 API stability + hardening]\n27\t    E002 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 Docs uplift]\n28\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 Support posture] --> E006\n29\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 TrustyAI guardrails supported] --> E006\n30\t    E005[RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 Dashboard default enablement] --> E006\n31\t```\n32\t\n33\t## DAG Justification\n34\t\n35\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n36\t|------|------|-----------|\n37\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The GA scope (size and content of the stability and hardening work) is explicitly unknown until the assessment lands — the strategy's only open question states this. E002 is a true gate with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`: the assessment outcome rewrites E002's scope. The outcome space is an unbounded gap inventory, not ≤3 bounded possibilities, so no conditional branch epics; the rewrite impact is recorded on E002. |\n38\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Migration notes and GA-standard docs must describe the API stability guarantees, deprecation policy, and upgrade path that E002 delivers. |\n39\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 | Docs must reflect the published SLO targets, backport policy, and supported configuration matrix. |\n40\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 | Docs must describe the TrustyAI guardrails integration as supported, matching what E004 delivers. |\n41\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 | Docs must document default enablement behavior for entitled clusters as shipped by E005. |\n42\t| (no edge) E001 → E003 | — | The support-posture deliverables (SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix) are defined independently of the gap inventory; the assessment's enumerated areas (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling) do not change whether these deliverables exist. The supported configuration matrix is validated against current TP behavior; any scale-limit findings from the assessment are folded in as an acceptance criterion, not a DAG edge. |\n43\t| (no edge) E002 ↔ E003 | Rule 15 / work-product split | Same component and team, but different work-product types (code stability/hardening vs. support policy and configuration-matrix validation) with different review cycles. No shared artifact: parallel. |\n44\t| (no edge) E002 ↔ E004, E002 ↔ E005 | Rule 14 | Different repos, no shared build artifacts. Dashboard builds against the existing LlamaStackDistribution CRD and Kubernetes API (per architecture context), not against E002's outputs. |\n45\t| (no edge) E001 → E004, E001 → E005 | — | HLR-4 and HLR-5 are fully defined deliverables whose existence and shape do not depend on the assessment outcome. |\n46\t\n47\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n48\t\n49\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n50\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n51\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | GA-readiness assessment producing the definitive GA scope. |\n52\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases, plus assessment-driven hardening (error handling, scale limits). |\n53\t| HLR-3 | P0 | E003 | Documented SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix. |\n54\t| HLR-4 | P1 | E004 | TrustyAI-owned epic; the integration spans TrustyAI and Llama Stack distributions — assigned to TrustyAI as the most-relevant canonical component (see Component Mapping). |\n55\t| HLR-5 | P1 | E005 | Default enablement for entitled clusters, removing the TP opt-in gate. |\n56\t| HLR-6 | P2 | E006 | docs-authoring epic; priority P0 derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` per the docs-authoring priority exception (Step 4), not from HLR-6's P2 label. It does not push priority upstream — E004 and E005 remain P1. |\n57\t\n58\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements, no bundled lower-priority HLRs.\n59\t\n60\t## Component Mapping\n61\t\n62\t- All strategy components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard) exist in `.context/architecture-context/` (rhoai-3.0) — no new-component onboarding chains are needed; all are Konflux-onboarded per their context files, so no konflux-onboarding or license-validation epics apply.\n63\t- Canonical component names used verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`: `Llama Stack`, `TrustyAI`, `Dashboard`, `Documentation`.\n64\t- HLR-4's integration work spans TrustyAI (guardrails services) and Llama Stack (safety surface in the distribution). Assigned the single canonical component `TrustyAI` per the one-component-per-epic constraint; the TrustyAI team owns the supported-integration deliverable. If Llama Stack-distribution-side wiring changes turn out to be required, they are coordinated with RHOAI GenAI within that epic's scope rather than a separate epic (the strategy treats the integration as one capability).\n65\t- E006 uses canonical component `Documentation` (the docs work covers Llama Stack GA content; assigned to RHOAI GenAI, the owning team of the graduated component, since the strategy names no docs team).\n66\t\n67\t## Health Warnings\n68\t\n69\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements, and no Staff Engineer Input section. Owner teams were taken from `.context/architecture-context/` (RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI, RHOAI Dashboard); acceptance criteria below were derived from HLR text. Non-blocking; verify team assignments with the strategy author.\n70\t- **Epic kept above the ~2-week guideline (E002)**: estimated 4–6 weeks of stability and hardening work. It is kept as one epic because it is a single logical change over one API surface set — API versioning, deprecation policy, and the upgrade path share the same call sites and would force a second pass over the same surfaces if split, and the assessment-driven hardening cannot be bounded before the assessment lands. **Conditional re-split trigger**: once the GA-readiness assessment delivers its gap inventory, re-split E002 by gap category (API stability vs. error handling vs. scale limits) if the remediation inventory exceeds the strategy's estimate or exceeds one team's ~2-week capacity.\n71\t- **Priority inversion note (accepted by rule)**: E006 is P0 (docs-authoring exception: strategy `priority: Critical`) even though HLR-6 is P2. The exception also prevents E006 from pushing P0 onto E004/E005.\n72\t- **Release-train coordination, not a DAG dependency**: Dashboard default enablement (E005) and the GA-quality work (E002) should land in the same release