{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc67499712cc0d1c","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9014\n3\tepic_count: 6\n4\tcritical_path_length: 3\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\trevised: true\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9014: Graduate Llama Stack to General Availability\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Component | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|-----------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 | GA-readiness assessment for Llama Stack | Investigation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 | Llama Stack API stability guarantee and GA hardening | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 | Llama Stack production support posture | Implementation | RHOAI GenAI | Llama Stack | P0 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 | TrustyAI guardrails as a supported Llama Stack integration | Implementation | TrustyAI | TrustyAI | P1 |\n20\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 | Dashboard Llama Stack management enabled by default | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | Dashboard | P1 |\n21\t| RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 | Llama Stack GA documentation uplift and TP migration notes | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI GenAI | Documentation | P0 |\n22\t\n23\t## Dependency DAG\n24\t\n25\t```mermaid\n26\tgraph TD\n27\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9014-E001 GA-readiness assessment] --> E002[RHAISTRAT-9014-E002 API stability + hardening]\n28\t    E002 --> E003[RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 Support posture]\n29\t    E002 --> E006[RHAISTRAT-9014-E006 Docs uplift]\n30\t    E003[RHAISTRAT-9014-E003 Support posture] --> E006\n31\t    E004[RHAISTRAT-9014-E004 TrustyAI guardrails supported] --> E006\n32\t    E005[RHAISTRAT-9014-E005 Dashboard default enablement] --> E006\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\t## DAG Justification\n36\t\n37\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n38\t|------|------|-----------|\n39\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates downstream) | The GA scope (size and content of the stability and hardening work) is explicitly unknown until the assessment lands — the strategy's only open question states this. E002 is a true gate with `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`: the assessment outcome rewrites E002's scope. The outcome space is an unbounded gap inventory, not ≤3 bounded possibilities, so no conditional branch epics; the rewrite impact is recorded on E002. |\n40\t| E002 → E006 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Migration notes and GA-standard docs must describe the API stability guarantees, deprecation policy, and upgrade path that E002 delivers. |\n41\t| E003 → E006 | Rule 11 | Docs must reflect the published SLO targets, backport policy, and supported configuration matrix. |\n42\t| E004 → E006 | Rule 11 | Docs must describe the TrustyAI guardrails integration as supported, matching what E004 delivers. |\n43\t| E005 → E006 | Rule 11 | Docs must document default enablement behavior for entitled clusters as shipped by E005. |\n44\t| E002 → E003 | Shared artifact (Rule 15 exception) | E002's acceptance criteria hand validated scale limits to the support-posture work for inclusion in the supported configuration matrix, and E003's scope/ACs incorporate those handed-over limits — a concrete deliverable passed between the epics, so the same component/team parallel-by-default rule does not apply. This edge also orders E003 after E001 transitively (E001 → E002 → E003), covering the GA-scope alignment in E003's scope. |\n45\t| (no edge) E001 → E003 | — (covered transitively) | The support-posture deliverables (SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix) are defined independently of the gap inventory; the assessment's enumerated areas (API stability, upgrade paths, scale limits, error handling) do not change whether these deliverables exist. The alignment of the matrix with the GA scope defined by the readiness assessment is covered transitively by the E002 → E003 edge. |\n46\t| (no edge) E002 ↔ E004, E002 ↔ E005 | Rule 14 | Different repos, no shared build artifacts. Dashboard builds against the existing LlamaStackDistribution CRD and Kubernetes API (per architecture context), not against E002's outputs. |\n47\t| (no edge) E001 → E004, E001 → E005 | — | HLR-4 and HLR-5 are fully defined deliverables whose existence and shape do not depend on the assessment outcome. |\n48\t\n49\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n50\t\n51\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n52\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n53\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 | GA-readiness assessment producing the definitive GA scope. |\n54\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | Versioned APIs, deprecation policy, tested upgrade path from last two TP releases, plus assessment-driven hardening (error handling, scale limits). |\n55\t| HLR-3 | P0 | E003 | Documented SLO targets, backport policy, supported configuration matrix. |\n56\t| HLR-4 | P1 | E004 | TrustyAI-owned epic; the integration spans TrustyAI and Llama Stack distributions — assigned to TrustyAI as the most-relevant canonical component (see Component Mapping). |\n57\t| HLR-5 | P1 | E005 | Default enablement for entitled clusters, removing the TP opt-in gate. |\n58\t| HLR-6 | P2 | E006 | docs-authoring epic; priority P0 derived from strategy frontmatter `priority: Critical` per the docs-authoring priority exception (Step 4), not from HLR-6's P2 label. It does not push priority upstream — E004 and E005 remain P1. |\n59\t\n60\tAll six HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements, no bundled lower-priority HLRs.\n61\t\n62\t## Component Mapping\n63\t\n64\t- All strategy components (Llama Stack, TrustyAI, Dashboard) exist in `.context/architecture-context/` (rhoai-3.0) — no new-component onboarding chains are needed; all are Konflux-onboarded per their context files, so no konflux-onboarding or license-validation epics apply.\n65\t- Canonical component names used verbatim from `.context/rhai-components.txt`: `Llama Stack`, `TrustyAI`, `Dashboard`, `Documentation`.\n66\t- HLR-4's integration work spans TrustyAI (guardrails services) and Llama Stack (safety surface in the distribution). Assigned the single canonical component `TrustyAI` per the one-component-per-epic constraint; the TrustyAI team owns the supported-integration deliverable. If Llama Stack-distribution-side wiring changes turn out to be required, they are coordinated with RHOAI GenAI within that epic's scope rather than a separate epic (the strategy treats the integration as one capability).\n67\t- E006 uses canonical component `Documentation` (the docs work covers Llama Stack GA content; assigned to RHOAI GenAI, the owning team of the graduated component, since the strategy names no docs team).\n68\t\n69\t## Health Warnings\n70\t\n71\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy has no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements, and no Staff Engineer Input section. Owner teams were taken from `.context/architecture-context/` (RHOAI GenAI, TrustyAI, RHOAI Dashboard); acceptance criteria below were derived from HLR text. Non-blocking; verify team assignments with the strategy author.\n72\t- **Epic kept above the ~2-week guideline (E002)**: estimated 4–6 weeks of stability and hardening work. It is kept as one epic because it is a single logical change over one API surface set — API versioning, deprecation policy, and the upgrade path share the same call sites and would force a second pass over the same surfaces if split, and the assessment-driven hardening cannot be bounded before the assessment lands. **Conditional re-split trigger**: once the GA-readiness assessment delivers its gap inventory, re-split E002 by gap category (API stability vs. error handling vs. scale limits) if the remediation inventory exceeds the strategy's estimate or exceeds one team's ~2-week capacity.\n73\t- **Priority inversion note (accepted by rule)**: E006 is P0 (docs-authoring exception: strategy `priority: Critical`) even though HLR-6 is P2. The exception also prevents E006 from pushing P0 onto E004/E005.\n74\t- **Release-train coordination, not a DAG dependency**: Dashboard default enablement (E005) and the GA-quality work (E002) should land in the same release so users are not defaulted onto a pre-GA component. This is scheduling coordination recorded here, deliberately not a `dependencies` edge — the Dashboard work builds against the existing CRD and Kubernetes API.\n75\t\n76\t## Tiered Delivery\n77\t\n78\tNot applicable. No external dependency with uncertain timing exists: all work is internal to the platform's teams and repos; the single unknown (GA scope) is resolved by an internal Investigation, not an upstream/vendor dependency.\n79\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aaec3bbc2be06058","agent_id":"a602e19a0ce932531","session_id":"ee65eb59-0a07-4c88-ab7a-b3f887908170","uuid":"3972d162-9b6a-4606-9070-be7200c768eb","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:23:47.682Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bc67499712cc0d1c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Re-review RHAISTRAT-9014 decomposition"}
